const title = 'USPS Form 1583 Online Notarization: What USPS Requires and What Each Provider Charges';
const description = 'Since May 2024 USPS lets you sign Form 1583 on a live video call with the mail center or a U.S.-commissioned notary. Which virtual mailbox providers need a notary, what their online notary costs, and which IDs to have ready.';
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":"2026-11-22"};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <h1>USPS Form 1583 and online notarization</h1>
  <div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> No, USPS doesn't require a notary. Since May 1, 2024, you can sign Form 1583 on a live video call with the mail center's staff or before a U.S.-commissioned notary. Traveling Mailbox, Stable and US Global Mail verify you without a notary; iPostal1's online notary costs $25 and PostScan Mail's partner notary $20. <Src href={S.uspsBulletin} /><Src href={S.tmbKb} /><Src href={S.ipFaq} /><Src href={S.psmFaq} /></p></div>
  <p class="lede">Form 1583, "Application for Delivery of Mail Through Agent," is the form that lets a virtual mailbox company receive your mail. Whether you need a notary depends on the provider, not on USPS.</p>

  <h2>Does USPS require a notary for Form 1583?</h2>
  <p>A USPS rule change took effect on May 1, 2024. The applicant must sign in one of two ways:</p>
  <blockquote>"sign or confirm his or her signature in the physical or virtual (in real-time audio and video) presence of the CMRA owner or manager or authorized employee, or acknowledge his or her signature in the physical or virtual (in real-time audio and video) presence of a notary public commissioned in a United States state, territory, possession, or the District of Columbia." <Src href={S.uspsBulletin} label="USPS Postal Bulletin" /></blockquote>
  <p>So a live video call with the mail center's own staff is enough under USPS rules. A notary is one option, not a requirement, and if you do use one, the notary must hold a U.S. commission <Src href={S.uspsBulletin} label="USPS" />. The current form says the same thing <Src href={S.usps1583} label="PS 1583" />.</p>

  <h2>Which providers need a notary?</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Notary needed?</th><th>Cost to sign</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Traveling Mailbox</td><td>No</td><td>Included</td><td>Fill out the form in your account, then do a video appointment where staff check your IDs and you e-sign. "A notary is not required." <Src href={S.tmbKb} /> <Src href={S.tmb1583} label="2" /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stable</td><td>No</td><td>Included</td><td>Stable pre-fills the form and checks your IDs and an ID verification photo, which takes "about 1 business day or less" <Src href={S.stable1583} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>US Global Mail</td><td>No</td><td>Included</td><td>Pre-filled form. "No need to find a notary separately — we'll complete your identity verification online." <Src href={S.usgm1583} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Earth Class Mail (LegalZoom)</td><td>Optional</td><td>LegalZoom calls its notary option a "free notarization service" (2026-11-21) <Src href={S.lzPersonal} /></td><td>Verify yourself online (ID photos, a selfie, e-sign) or use a partner online notary. Without a Social Security number, you have to use the notary <Src href={S.ecm1583} />. Billing "starts upon account creation" whether or not the form is approved <Src href={S.ecmNonSsn} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>iPostal1</td><td>Yes, unless you sign in person at the mail center</td><td>$25 online notary</td><td>Many mail centers let you sign in person (some charge). Customers outside the U.S. must use the online notary <Src href={S.ipFaq} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>PostScan Mail</td><td>Yes</td><td>$20 partner online notary</td><td>Notarize online or in person, then submit through the platform <Src href={S.psmFaq} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Anytime Mailbox</td><td>Yes</td><td>—</td><td>Upload two IDs, then "complete and notarize" the form. The "Use Online Notary" button routes you to a video call with a licensed notary <Src href={S.atmFaq} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>PhysicalAddress.com</td><td>Yes, unless you sign at one of its locations</td><td>Not published</td><td>After signup it emails a pre-filled form and a link to a partner online notary that "supports international clients." You can also sign with a notary or in person at one of its locations. Mail is not scanned, forwarded or uploaded until the form is completed <Src href={S.paSignup} /> <Src href={S.pa1583} label="1583" /></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Which IDs do you need for Form 1583?</h2>
  <p>You need two IDs: one with your photo and one showing your current address <Src href={S.atmFaq} />. PostScan Mail's list is typical:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photo ID:</strong> U.S. driver's license or state ID, passport or passport card, permanent resident card, certificate of naturalization, U.S. military or university ID, and a few others <Src href={S.psmFaq} /></li>
    <li><strong>Address ID:</strong> driver's license (if you didn't use it as your photo ID), voter or vehicle registration, vehicle or home insurance policy, lease, mortgage or deed of trust <Src href={S.psmFaq} /></li>
  </ul>
  <p>Not accepted: Social Security cards, credit cards and birth certificates <Src href={S.atmFaq} />. One license can't count as both IDs <Src href={S.psmFaq} />.</p>

  <h2>Things that trip people up</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Form 1583 doesn't forward your mail.</strong> It only authorizes the mail center. You still have to file a change of address or update your senders <Src href={S.tmb1583} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Every name needs its own form.</strong> Traveling Mailbox requires a form and two IDs "for each name that mail will be received under" <Src href={S.tmb1583} />. PostScan charges $5 a month per extra recipient <Src href={S.psmFaq} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Switching locations means a new form.</strong> Traveling Mailbox requires a new Form 1583 for every recipient when you move to a different location <Src href="https://kb.travelingmailbox.com/do-i-need-a-new-usps-form-1583-when-switching-addresses/" />.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>An independent online notary</h2>
  <p>If your provider requires a notarized form, some send you to their own partner and others let you bring a form that was notarized separately. Confirm which yours accepts before you pay.</p>
  <p>BlueNotary is an online notary that says it can notarize Form 1583 over a live video session: you upload the form and an ID, then meet a commissioned online notary. It says a notary acknowledgment can take less than 10 minutes <Src href={S.bn1583} />. Its fee schedule lists on-demand notarization at $25 for one stamp <Src href={S.bnFees} />.</p>
  <p>Notarize, which now operates under the Proof name, also offers online notarization of Form 1583: you upload the form, verify your identity, and meet a notary on a live video call. Its Form 1583 page lists the first notary seal at $25 and says signers complete their notarization in less than 10 minutes on average, with notaries available 24/7 <Src href={S.notarize1583} />.</p>

